  "answer_text": "In short, **ambrosia is primarily considered a food**, but depending on whether you are looking at classical mythology or modern cuisine, it can also refer to a drink. \n\n### 1. In Greek Mythology\nIn ancient Greek myths, the standard and most common distinction is:\n* **Ambrosia** is the **food** of the gods.\n* **Nectar** is the **drink** of the gods.\n\nTogether, they granted the Olympian gods their eternal youth and immortality. \n\nHowever, ancient writers did not always agree, and the two terms were sometimes used interchangeably:\n* The poet **Homer** established the standard of ambrosia as food and nectar as drink.\n* The poet **Sappho** and the playwright **Anaxandrides** described ambrosia as a drink, while the poet **Alcman** wrote that nectar was the food.\n* Some historians believe the original divine substance they were based on was honey (or mead, which is fermented honey). Because honey can be eaten as a solid food or diluted and consumed as a beverage, the distinction was naturally blurry.\n* Beyond consumption, the gods also used liquid ambrosia as a healing salve, a perfume, or an anointing oil to wash away mortality.\n\n### 2. In Modern Cuisine\nToday, **ambrosia** is strictly a **food**. Specifically, \"ambrosia salad\" is a classic American dessert salad. It is a sweet food made from a mixture of:\n* Mandarin oranges and pineapple\n* Shredded coconut\n* Miniature marshmallows\n* Whipped cream, cool whip, sour cream, or yogurt \n\nSo, if you are reading classical Greek poetry, you might occasionally see it referred to as a drink, but in almost all other contexts\u2014both mythological and culinary\u2014**ambrosia is a food**.",
